WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:08.220
For over 60 years,
policymakers have pushed training in

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.450
STEM fields. But even after decades of
investments to increase STEM credentials

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:16.850
and degrees, employers continue to say
workers don't have the skills they need.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:21.449
Research and employer surveys suggest
that many of the missing degrees can be

00:00:21.449 --> 00:00:26.130
found in non-cognitive or soft skills:
things like the ability to communicate,

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:31.289
solve problems, and empathize with others.
While they don't show up on standardized

00:00:31.289 --> 00:00:34.850
tests, these soft skills are critical to
life in the workforce.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:39.300
Research shows that investments in
family formation, early childhood

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.829
development, and job training programs
that integrate technical and soft skills

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:48.480
are the keys to expanding economic
opportunity, especially for those from

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.710
disadvantaged backgrounds. Time and again,
we've pushed stem training while not

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:56.820
achieving the success we expected.
Prioritizing policies that support

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.059
healthy families and encourage soft
skill development in childhood,

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:05.360
adolescence, and among young adults, might
be just the solution we're looking for.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.860
Where do you think soft skills come from,
and how do we get more of them? Let us

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:13.560
know in your comments. Also, let us know
what other topics you'd like our

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.299
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be
sure to LIKE and subscribe for more

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:22.310
research and videos from AEI.

